      "faqs": [
        {
          "question": "Can my loved one apply if their house is worth more than $75,000?",
          "answer": "Yes, the $75,000 limit applies only to countable assets like cash, bank accounts, and stocks. Your loved one's primary home is excluded from the asset calculation, regardless of value. However, any secondary properties or rental properties do count toward the $75,000 limit."
        },
        {
          "question": "What happens if my loved one sells the house before the modifications are complete?",
          "answer": "The loan becomes immediately due upon sale, refinance, or transfer of the property. If modifications are in progress, your loved one would need to repay whatever loan funds have been disbursed at closing. This is why timing the modifications with long-term housing plans is important."
        },
        {
          "question": "Can we use any contractor or does it have to be someone specific?",
          "answer": "You can choose any licensed and insured Massachusetts contractor, but they must complete the official Home Modification Loan Program Bid Form and Scope of Work Bid Form. The contractor's license and insurance must be verified and current. No loan funds are released without these completed contractor forms."
        },
        {
          "question": "How much income is too much to qualify?",
          "answer": "Income limits are set at 200% of Area Median Income and vary by household size and region. For example, this might be $70,000 annually for a single person in some areas or $120,000 for a larger household in high-cost regions. Contact your regional provider for current dollar limits in your specific area."
        },
        {
          "question": "What if my loved one needs both accessibility modifications and regular home repairs?",
          "answer": "This program only covers accessibility modifications tied to documented disabilities or functional limitations. It excludes general repairs like roofing, windows, heating systems, or septic work. Your loved one would need to fund regular repairs separately or look into other home repair assistance programs."
        },
        {
          "question": "Can I apply on behalf of my loved one who has dementia?",
          "answer": "If you have legal authority to act on your loved one's behalf (power of attorney or guardianship), you can apply for them. The property must still be in your loved one's name and they must meet all eligibility requirements. Include your legal documentation with the application."
        },
        {
          "question": "What happens if my loved one's income or health changes after approval?",
          "answer": "Once approved and modifications are complete, changes in income or health don't affect the existing loan. The 0% interest rate and deferred payment terms remain the same until the property is sold, refinanced, or transferred. However, any additional modification requests would require a new application and current eligibility verification."
        },
        {
          "question": "How long do we have to complete the modifications once approved?",
          "answer": "The sources don't specify a completion timeline, but loan funds are typically released in stages as work progresses. Contact your regional provider about timeline expectations and whether there are deadlines for starting or completing approved modifications."
        }
      ],
