      "intro": "If your parent is 65 or older, blind, or has a qualifying disability and lives in Massachusetts, MassHealth Standard is the most comprehensive Medicaid coverage available to them. It covers doctor visits, inpatient hospital stays, dental care, vision care, eyeglasses, hearing aids, prescription drugs, lab tests, physical therapy, and medical equipment. It also covers home health services, adult day health programs, mental health and addiction treatment, and, when the time comes, long-term care in a facility. This is not a supplemental plan. For many seniors who do not have Medicare, it is their primary and only coverage.\n\nEligibility depends on three things: category (your parent must qualify as aged 65+, blind, or disabled), countable income (MassHealth looks at income after deductions, not gross pay), and countable assets (the limit is $2,000 for one person and $3,000 for a couple). The income standard varies by category. For 2026, MassHealth publishes a monthly standard of $1,143.74 for blind applicants and $1,108.39 for disabled applicants. Seniors applying under the aged category should confirm the current standard directly with MassHealth, as it follows its own rule set. If your parent is in a long-term care facility, a separate income standard of $72.80 per month applies to what they keep as a personal needs allowance.\n\nThis program is not the same as ConnectorCare or a Medicare plan. If your parent already has Medicare, MassHealth Standard can work as secondary coverage, picking up costs Medicare does not cover. But many seniors qualify for MassHealth Standard regardless of whether they have Medicare. The application process for seniors and people needing long-term care uses a specific form, not the standard under-65 MassHealth application, and the senior/long-term-care route is the correct path for most families in this situation.",
