      "intro": "Massachusetts LIHEAP, also called the Fuel Assistance or HEAP program, helps lower-income households pay heating costs from November through April. If your parent lives alone and earns less than $53,585 per year (roughly $4,465 per month gross), they likely meet the income threshold. The standard heating benefit for FY2026 ranges from $200 to $600, paid directly to the heating provider so your parent never has to manage a reimbursement.\n\nThe program has three separate parts you should know about: regular heating assistance (up to $600), a crisis benefit (up to $600 if heat service is at risk of shutoff or runs out), and weatherization assistance (up to $18,500 for home insulation, sealing, and efficiency upgrades that lower bills long-term). Each component has its own eligibility trigger, so your parent may qualify for more than one at the same time.\n\nApplications go through the fuel assistance agency for your parent's specific city or town, not a single statewide office. That means phone numbers, appointment availability, and wait times vary by location. The heating season application window runs November 1 through April 30. Applying early in November gives the most flexibility if your parent's agency requires an in-person appointment.",
