      "intro": "If your parent is 65 or older and owns or rents a home in Massachusetts, they may qualify for a refundable state tax credit worth up to $2,820 for tax year 2025. Refundable means if the credit exceeds what they owe in state taxes, Massachusetts sends them the difference as a check or direct deposit. It is not just a reduction in what they owe; it can put real money back in their pocket.\n\nThis credit is available to homeowners and renters alike. Income limits are generous: up to $75,000 for a single filer, $94,000 for a head of household, and $112,000 for a married couple filing jointly. There is no asset test. The credit is claimed by filing Schedule CB with a Massachusetts income tax return, which your parent may need to file even if they normally skip it because of low income.\n\nThe biggest reason families miss this credit is not knowing it exists, or assuming it only applies to people who already file state taxes. If your parent has not claimed this in recent years, Massachusetts allows amended returns going back three years. A missed credit from 2022, 2023, and 2024 could mean thousands of dollars your family never collected.",
